Subject: [PATCH] venus: host-visible memory from AHardwareBuffers on Android

A driver with no dma-buf export, no usable opaque-fd export and no dma-buf import (or no
dma-buf heap to allocate from) leaves virglrenderer with no way to share host-visible memory
with the client: vtest takes only dma-buf and shm fds. Qualcomm's Adreno drivers are such
drivers: they have no VK_EXT_external_memory_dma_buf, and they export opaque fds of dedicated
allocations only (DEDICATED_ONLY), so vkGetMemoryFdKHR of the export path fails. This is so on the
Adreno 610 (Vulkan 1.1.128) and the Adreno 650 (Vulkan 1.1.128, driver build c095b0f6a5).

Such a driver imports AHardwareBuffer BLOBs into a host-visible coherent type
(VK_ANDROID_external_memory_android_hardware_buffer), and the buffer's first handle fd is a
dma-buf that another process maps coherently with the GPU (checked with vk-memprobe on the
Adreno 650: a CPU write through that mapping, a GPU fill and a GPU copy all read back in full,
with the CPU usage OFTEN and RARELY).

- An opaque-fd export that the driver allows only for dedicated allocations no longer counts as
  supported: the export path allocates without a dedicated buffer or image.
- On Android, with no dma-buf export, no opaque-fd export, no gbm and no dma-buf heap, and with
  VK_ANDROID_external_memory_android_hardware_buffer and VK_EXT_queue_family_foreign, the
  physical device takes the AHardwareBuffer path, and vkCreateDevice enables those two
  extensions (when the client did not).
- vkAllocateMemory of a host-visible type then allocates the page-rounded size as a BLOB
  (GPU_DATA_BUFFER; CPU usage OFTEN for a HOST_CACHED type, else RARELY), imports it with
  VkImportAndroidHardwareBufferInfoANDROID into the requested type or, when the buffer cannot
  go there, a host-visible type of the same heap that it can, and keeps the buffer's dma-buf
  (from AHardwareBuffer_sendHandleToUnixSocket over a socket pair; the NDK has no other way to
  read the handle) for the blob export, as the udmabuf path keeps its udmabuf. The buffer is
  released with the memory. A BLOB backs buffers only: an allocation dedicated to an image loses
  its VkMemoryDedicatedAllocateInfo when the driver does not require it for that image
  (vkGetImageMemoryRequirements2), and fails when it does.
- The type an import goes into, here and in the dma-buf heap path (patch 0002), is one pick
  (vkr_pick_import_memory_type): the requested type, else a host-visible type of the same heap
  that the import allows, best with the same HOST_* flags, else with the same HOST_CACHED; on
  the AHardwareBuffer path a coherent request takes only a coherent type (the client does no
  flush for it; on the heap path the uncached heap keeps the mapping coherent, and a driver may
  import a dma-buf into its cached type only). It reads the client's memory table with the driver's index shifted by one where
  the table is spoofed (mem_type_spoof); patch 0002 read the spoofed table with the driver's
  index. The AHardwareBuffer's CPU usage, so the client's mapping, follows the type it goes into
  (allocated again once when that differs from the requested type's HOST_CACHED).
- vkGetMemoryResourcePropertiesMESA answers VK_ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE when the driver
  has no VK_EXT_external_memory_dma_buf, instead of asking the driver about a handle type it
  does not know.

The server links libandroid (android/devdriver/build.sh). Written from virglrenderer main
e18f8c00's export, udmabuf and Metal paths (src/venus/vkr_device_memory.c,
vkr_physical_device.c, vkr_device.c), the Vulkan specification
(VK_ANDROID_external_memory_android_hardware_buffer: vkGetAndroidHardwareBufferPropertiesANDROID,
VkImportAndroidHardwareBufferInfoANDROID; VkExternalMemoryFeatureFlagBits) and the NDK's
android/hardware_buffer.h (AHardwareBuffer_allocate, AHardwareBuffer_sendHandleToUnixSocket).

